  "result": "`satisfies(version, range)` is implemented in `src/semver.js` per `docs/SPEC.md`, and `npm test` passes (56 passed, 0 failed). The new tests are in `test/semver.test.js`. They cover invalid input, version syntax, ordering, every table in the spec, `||` unions, and the prerelease rule.\n\nI made a few choices where the spec is silent:\n- **Operator spacing:** Whitespace between an operator and its version is accepted only for `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `=`. `~ 1.2.3` and `^ 1.2.3` return `false`, since the spec only allows the space for comparators.\n- **Wildcards and prereleases in ranges:** A wildcard followed by a concrete number (`1.x.3`) returns `false`, and so does a prerelease on a partial version (`1.2.x-beta`).\n- **Always-false comparators:** `>*` and `<*` match nothing.\n- **Empty sets:** An empty set around `||` is treated as \"any version\".\n\nI also wrote one test that was wrong, and I fixed the test rather than the code. It expected `1.0.0-alpha` to satisfy `<1.0.0`, but the spec's prerelease rule says it doesn't, because no comparator in that set has a prerelease on `1.0.0`. The ordering test now skips that case.\n\nI didn't call the advisor. The spec was explicit enough that I implemented directly. Nothing is committed.",
